LS(1)                          P32W Unix                           LS(1)

NAME
       ls - list directories and files

SYNOPSIS
       ls [-1ACFRSadhilnrst] [name ...]

DESCRIPTION
       For each directory named, ls lists its entries; for each
       other file, its name. With no name it lists the current
       directory. Entries whose names start with . are left out
       unless -a or -A is given. Entries are sorted by name (byte
       order, so capitals come first) unless -t or -S is given.

       When the standard output is a terminal the names are set in
       columns, sorted down each column and then across, as wide
       as the terminal (ttysize(3): its size if the system knows
       it, as in a window of wm(1); else $COLUMNS; else 80
       characters); otherwise, one name per line.

       With several names, the files come first, sorted, then each
       directory in the order given, under a heading dir: and
       separated by blank lines. A heading is also printed for a
       single directory if any other name could not be found.

       The long format (-l) shows, for each file:

       -rw-r--r--  1 ken      staff        123 Oct 14 10:40 hello.c

       the type (- file, d directory, c character device, b block
       device, p pipe) and the permissions (s for set-user-id or
       set-group-id with execute, S without; t for the sticky bit
       with execute, T without); the number of links; the owner
       and the group, by name (by number with -n, or when there is
       no name); the size in bytes, or for a device its major and
       minor numbers; and the time of last modification, local time
       (see tz(5)). A time within the last six months shows hour
       and minute; an older one, or one more than an hour in the
       future, shows the year instead. When a directory is listed
       in the long format, a first line total n gives the space its
       entries take, in KiB.

OPTIONS
       -1     one name per line, even on a terminal.
       -A     like -a, but without . and ..
       -C     columns, even when not writing to a terminal.
       -F     a mark after each name: / directory, * executable
              file, | pipe.
       -R     list each subdirectory too, recursively, under its
              own heading (. and .. are not followed).
       -S     sort by size, largest first.
       -a     list every entry, including those starting with .
       -d     list a directory itself, not its contents.
       -h     sizes (with -l, -s and in the total line) in human
              units: 999, 1.5K, 37K, 2.1M, rounded up.
       -i     the inode number before each name.
       -l     the long format.
       -n     the long format, with numeric user and group ids.
       -r     reverse the order of the sort.
       -s     before each name, the space it takes in KiB (the size
              rounded up to whole KiB; 0 for devices).
       -t     sort by modification time, newest first.

       Options may be grouped (-lt) or separate, and must come
       before the names; -- ends them. Of -1 and -C the last wins.
       With -t and -S together, -t sorts first and -S breaks ties;
       ties are otherwise broken by name.

EXAMPLES
       ls                     the current directory, in columns
       ls -l /usr/ken         long listing
       ls -lt | head          the ten most recently changed
       ls -lSrh /bin          by size, smallest first, with K and M
       ls -ld /tmp            the directory itself: drwxrwxrwt
       ls -aF                 everything, with type marks
       ls -R /usr             all of /usr

ENVIRONMENT
       COLUMNS        the width for columns, where the terminal's
                      is not known (default 80)
       TZ             the time zone for -l (see tz(5))

FILES
       /etc/passwd, /etc/group
                      the names of owners and groups

EXIT STATUS
       0 if all went well; 1 if a name or an entry could not be
       found or a directory could not be read (ls: name: and the
       reason; ls goes on with the rest); 2 for an unknown option
       (with a usage line).

SEE ALSO
       du(1), find(1), stat(2), chmod(1), passwd(5), group(5),
       dirent(3)

BUGS
       There is no -c or -u (inodes keep no access time), no -g,
       -o, -x, -q, -L or -p, and no symbolic links to follow. -d
       with no name lists the contents of . rather than . itself.
       On a terminal whose size the system does not know (a
       line), set it with stty(1), or set COLUMNS. -s
       does not print a total line, only -l does. Directory
       arguments are not sorted. In the long format an entry that
       cannot be stat'ed is reported and left out.
